      "finalText": "Yes — every failure mode has a concrete fix, and the fixes converge on a single architecture. Here's the mapping:\n\n**FM-5 (eviction destroys load-bearing context)** → Fix: evict to the store, not to nowhere. When DSA drops a branch from Zone B, it shouldn't vanish — it gets a one-line tombstone with a rehydration pointer (`context_fetch` coordinates). The model knows what it doesn't know and can ask for it back. This is exactly what §8's rehydration metadata was designed for; it just wasn't wired into the eviction path.\n\n**FM-8 (cache-safe selection never fires)** → Fix: batch eviction at rare epochs instead of guarding per turn. Monotone lock-in isn't inherent to selection — it's the price of trying to select *every turn* without touching the prefix. The correct cadence: leave Zone B byte-frozen for long stretches, and when it exceeds a budget (~100+ branches), run selection **once**, rewrite Zone B once, eat one deliberate 1.25× cache epoch, then freeze again. The amortization works because the rewrite cost is paid once while 0.1× re-read savings accrue every subsequent turn.\n\n**Untrained selector makes bad picks** → Fix: let the LLM rank at the boundary. Since eviction now happens at a rare phase-boundary call anyway, the summarizer does the scoring — \"which of these 40 branches still bear on open work\" is a question the strong model answers far better than cosine similarity. Pin the active branch's ancestor path unconditionally; everything else is fair game.\n\nPut those together and you get **hierarchical Zone B collapse**: old branches fold into super-summaries (the tombstones), triggered by budget thresholds (the batching), chosen by the model (the trained selector). That's the fixed version of DSA-on-branches, just under a different name.\n\nTwo caveats before running it. First, at current scale this fixes a non-problem — v5.6 wins with a handful of branches and Zone B nowhere near budget. Second, any measurement inherits the standing prerequisite: pin temperature first, or the 10-vs-37-turn trajectory noise will bury whatever effect you're testing.",
